  "user_handle": "Pexy",
  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n### Root Cause\n\nIn `CLOB.sol` lines 503-504 and 544-545, the code uses a bitwise AND operator (`&`) instead of logical OR (`||`):\n\n```solidity\n// _processLimitBidOrder\nif (baseTokenAmountReceived != quoteTokenAmountSent && \n    baseTokenAmountReceived & quoteTokenAmountSent == 0) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n\n// _processLimitAskOrder  \nif (baseTokenAmountSent != quoteTokenAmountReceived &&\n    baseTokenAmountSent & quoteTokenAmountReceived == 0) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n```\n\nThe intent is to prevent zero-cost trades where one side receives tokens without sending any. However, the bitwise `&` operator causes false positives when legitimate trade amounts have no overlapping bits (e.g., 256 & 255 = 0).\n\n### Impact\n\nThe condition treats any two matched amounts with disjoint 1-bits as zero-cost trades. This affects ALL limit order matching (both partial and full matches), while fill orders remain unaffected as they use different validation logic.\n\nThe bug is deterministically reproducible. When matched base amount is a power-of-two (2^k) and matched quote is any value < 2^k, the bitwise AND equals zero by construction, triggering a false `ZeroCostTrade()` revert. Examples include:\n- base = 256, quote ∈ {1..255} → all revert\n- base = 512, quote ∈ {1..511} → all revert\n- base = 1024, quote ∈ {1..1023} → all revert\n\nBeyond power-of-two cases, any amounts with no overlapping bits trigger the bug (e.g., 40 & 21 = 0b101000 & 0b010101 = 0).\n\nThis causes legitimate trades to fail with a misleading error, damaging user experience and market efficiency. Traders, especially algorithmic ones using round numbers, will encounter unexplained failures.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nReplace the faulty condition with proper OR logic:\n\n```solidity\n// _processLimitBidOrder\nif (baseTokenAmountReceived == 0 || quoteTokenAmountSent == 0) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n\n// _processLimitAskOrder\nif (baseTokenAmountSent == 0 || quoteTokenAmountReceived == 0) {\n    revert ZeroCostTrade();\n}\n```\n\nRemove the `!=` comparison as it's meaningless between different token units.",
  "proof_of_concept": "The following PoC demonstrates the bug in the limit order path where it actually occurs:\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.27;\n\nimport {PoCTestBase} from \"./PoCTestBase.t.sol\";\n\nimport {Test, console} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {CLOBTestBase} from \"../clob/utils/CLOBTestBase.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\nimport {Side} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\n\ncontract PoC is CLOBTestBase {\n    function test_submissionValidity() public {\n        // Setup: Relax settings to trigger the bug\n        vm.startPrank(address(clobManager));\n        clob.setTickSize(1);                      // 1 wei price increments\n        clob.setMinLimitOrderAmountInBase(100);   // Minimum allowed\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Price = 255/256 * 1e18 (triggers 256 & 255 = 0)\n        uint256 price = (255 * 1e18) / 256;\n\n        address maker = users[0];\n        address taker = users[1];\n\n        // 1) Maker posts ASK: 256 base @ price\n        setupOrder(Side.SELL, maker, 256, price);\n\n        // 2) Taker posts BID: 256 base @ price (will match and trigger bug)\n        setupTokens(Side.BUY, taker, 256, price, true);\n\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory bidArgs = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: 256,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: 0,\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n\n        // Execute and expect ZeroCostTrade revert\n        vm.expectRevert(CLOB.ZeroCostTrade.selector);\n        vm.prank(taker);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(taker, bidArgs);\n\n        // The bug occurs because in _processLimitBidOrder:\n        // baseTokenAmountReceived = 256\n        // quoteTokenAmountSent = 255  \n        // (256 != 255) && (256 & 255 == 0) => true => false revert!\n    }\n}\n```\n\nOutput: Transaction reverts with `ZeroCostTrade()` despite being a legitimate trade of 256 base for 255 quote tokens.",
